NIST 800-160 identifies 30 principles for trustworthy secure design. many of these are well established practices used in a variety of disciplines. designing a system around these principles inevitably involves trade off in performance, operations, or support


NIST 800-160 identifies 30 specific processes arranged in four process groups that are performed during the system life cycle to achieve trustworthy systems




Technical Processes: business or mission analysis (BA), stakeholder needs and requirements definition (SN), system requirements definition (SR), system architecture definition (SA), design definition (DE), system analysis (SA), implementation (IP), integration (IN), verification (VE), transition (TR), validation (VA), operation (OP), maintenance (MA), disposal (DS)

Technical Management Process: project planning (PL), project assessment control (PA), decision management (DM), risk management (RM), configuration management (CM), information management (IM), measurement (MS), quality assurance (QA)

Organizational Project Enabling Process: life cycle model management (LC), infrastructure management (IM), portfolio management (PM), human resource management (HR), quality management (QM), knowledge management (KM)

Agreement Process: acquisition (AQ), supply (SP)





the vulnerability scanning processes should establish a baseline of vulnerabilities across the range of systems identified in the strategic scope. the initial results will be compared against organizational beelines to determine whether there is divergence in implementation from the documented configuration of the systems. these results should be logged, typically in a central repository, so the information they contain can be integrated into the agreed upon reporting process. the results of the assessment activities will be communicated to system owners in the manner agreed upon in the VM plan. this would typically identify the classification and the overall vulnerability classification of the system. summarized reporting based on the vulnerability classification scale would also be provided to the organizations senior leadership consistent with the performance metrics set for the program. 


conducting scans in a shared responsibility environment will usually require additional controls and communication with the various entities involved in providing the service. with cloud provider, there may be contractual limits on the conduct of scanning activities. in many cases, the service provider does not allow external scans of their environment. this puts more responsibility on the customer to ensure the vulnerability program of the vendor is addressed in the contract through service level agreements and information sharing practices.


when new systems are introduced into the environment, the vulnerability assessment tools, practices, and resources should be available to the development/deployment teams to ensure they know exactly how the system will be evaluated once deployed. many times, systems have been created in the development environment only to find that they fail to meet the current vulnerability standards in the production environment. the VM team needs to be informed of development activities early in the development cycle, ensuring that the appropriate controls are integrated into the Systems Security Plans and that unique vulnerability management requirements are identified. despite the expertise the VM brings to the development effort, it remains the responsibility of the system owner to ensure that newly deployed systems conform to the organizations VM program. 


the following figure shows the components of a vulnerability management plan. pen testing, code review, code scanner, automated vulnerability scanner, vendor disclosure, online databases, other sources


while organizational practices may differ, system owners are generally responsible for establishing remediation plans to address system vulnerabilities. in some cases, changes to common or hybrid controls may be necessary to compensate for an inability to remediate a systems vulnerabilities. the remediation plan should also include a timeline for each phase of the remediation process, detailing key milestones and deadlines. it is crucial to establish metrics for success to evaluate the effectiveness of the implemented remedies.


many organizations vulnerability classification systems require reporting of vulnerabilities at a certain level beyond the system owner. the sensitive nature of these communications must also be respected. accurate reporting is essential to maintain trust in the vulnerability management program


a vulnerability may be identified that cannot be exploited by a malicious actor. these "false positive" can skey the reporting of vulnerabilities, consuming effort to identify and document the error in the assessment tool, resulting in decreased confidence in the VM program as system owners expend resources addressing nonexistent problems


similarly, the VM tools may not identify a weakness (e.g. a software logic flaw). these "false negatives" may be due to a configuration issue with the tool or other technical problem, or they may be due to a zero day weakness that is not known when the scan is performed. regardless, the weakness exists, and once discovered by a bad actor exploiting the weakness or by someone reading about it in the press, the VM scanning process is unfairly accused of failing to identify a weakness. the fault lies not with the scanning tool but with the VM programs failure to adequately account for the risk of "unknown unknowns"


as with all processes, the VM program will benefit from continuous process improvement practices. based on the organizations VM strategy for continuous improvement, periodic reviews of the performance of the vulnerability scanning processes, remediation efforts and communication activities should be undertaken. changes to these programs should follow other data driven decision making practices embraced by the organization


it is essential to communicate transparently with stakeholders about the programs success and failures, while carefully handling the sensitive nature of the programs information 


