common security controls might apply to all organizational information systems or specific groups of systems either at specific locations or that use common methods, subsystems, or infrastructures. these common controls may be used within a single baseline or shared across multiple controls baselines. these common security controls are often identified in the organizations information security plans or as part of its information architecture and are included in the common security controls after working with authorizing officials, information systems owners, information system security officers, and other stakeholders.


these common security controls exhibit similar characteristics. the development, implementation, and assessment of common security controls can be assigned to specific responsible agency officials or organizational elements (other than the information system owners who systems will implement or use the common security controls), and the results from the assessment of the common security controls can be used to support the security authorization process for information systems where those controls have been applied


many of the management and operational controls (e.g. contingency planning, incident response, security awareness and training, personnel security, and physical security) needed to protect an information system may be excellent candidates for common security control status. the objective is to reduce security costs by centrally managing the development, implementation, and assessment of the common security controls designated by the organization. subsequently, sharing the assessment results with the owners of information systems where those common security controls are applied reduces the effort needed to authorize additional systems that rely on that control.


security controls not designat4ed as common controls are considered system specific controls, and it is the responsibility of the information system owner to implemented for their use. to prevent confusion, the organization should have clear architectural standards defining the common controls, so individual system security plans can identify which security controls have been designated as common security controls and which controls have been designated as system specific controls


for efficiency in developing system security plans, common security controls should be documented once and then inserted or imported into each system security plan for the information system within the agency, and the common control owner should be listed in the security plan. other factors that effectively maximize the application of common controls in the system security planning process include the following: developing, documenting, and communicating specific guidance identifying common security controls to system and control owners, developers, project managers, and others responsible for systems development, operation, or maintenance. assigning responsibility to an appropriate individual for coordinating common security control identification and review, along with processes for designating and managing common controls. establishing clear organizational practices for the selection and use of common controls, minimizing exceptions to their use, and identifying the system security planning process, which includes the use of common controls. properly resourcing and maintaining the common controls, including identifying organization experts in the common control areas to provide expertise to the users of common controls.


it is not unusual for a system to implement a system specific control and have subsequent system development efforts identify that control ass appropriate for the new system. in such cases, system owners should be consulted before the control is selected for use in the new system. if the control is determined to be applicable to the new system without degrading the operation of the existing system, then the control should be elevated to common control status and have a control owner assigned. all systems that rely on that control should have their systems security plans updated to reflect the new control. 


an agency may also assign a hybrid status to security controls. in these situations, part of the control is deemed to be a common control, while another part of the control is deemed to be system specific. for example, an agency may view the Incident Response Policy and Procedures security control as a hybrid control. the policy portion of the control is deemed a common control, and the procedures portion is deemed system specific


hybrid security controls may also serve as templates for further control refinement


an agency may choose, for example, to implement its Contingency Plan security control as a master template for a generalized contingency plan for all agency information systems. individual systems owners are responsible for tailoring the plan, where appropriate, to meet system specific issues.


assigned control owners are responsible for the development and implementation of the designated common security controls. they ensure that the required controls are assessed, and the assessment results are shared with the appropriate information systems owners. too often, organization designate common co9ntrols and then ignore their long term maintenance and support. for example, the domain name system (DNS) is a classic common control that virtually every TCP/IP network in the world uses. unfortunately, the adoption of secure DNSs by many organizations has lagged despite the well defined weaknesses of legacy DNS services. proper stewardship of those controls by a designated owner would, in many cases, speed up the adoption of more secure services.


partitioning security controls into common security controls and system specific security controls can result in significant savings for the agency in control development and implementation costs.


it can also result in a more consistent application of the security controls across the agency at large. moreover, equally significant savings can be realized in the security controls in every information system, the certification process draws on any applicable results from the most current assessment of the common security controls performed at the agency level. an agency wide approach to reuse and sharing of assessment results can greatly enhance the efficiency of the security certifications and accreditations being conducted by an agency and significantly reduce security program costs.



