system specific controls are the information systems owners responsibility to identify, implement, assess, operate, and sustain. however, the responsibility for these activities may be unclear without delineation.


an organizations common controls are designated to multiple systems. an individual is selected as the common control provider, responsible for the design, implementation, security assessment, operation, and maintenance of the control. depending on the control, the common provider may be responsible for deploying the control within the individual systems, but generally the system owner is responsible for integrating the common controls functions.


a common control is a security control inherited by one or more organizational information systems. this might be the organizations information security policies, which all the organization uses.


although many controls necessitate centralized coordination, the implementation of these controls is often the responsibility of the individual system owner


these are hybrid controls. for example, identity and access management services are typically offered as hybrid controls. the IAM system owner designs the IAM environment, selecting the services to be offered and the tools to be used.


a typical IAM deployment might include an X.500 compliant directory service, configured for sufficient redundancy and capacity to meet the projected needs of the organization. the IAM system owner defines the way that clients access the directory store, the hierarchical structure of the directory store, the data store schema, and the attributes to be populated. they are responsible for testing the directory service, deploying it in the production environment, monitoring the overall performance to ensure it meets specifications, and communicating the expected performance characteristics of the system with other system owners.


the other system owners are then responsible for implementing the X.500 clients for their systems, populating the directory with the relevant identities. the system owner then tests the operation of their clients and identities with their system to ensure they are properly presented and that assigned privileges operate as expected. both the IAM system owner and individual system owner who relies on it have responsibilities to the control, and both are responsible for resourcing, maintaining, and operating their part of the control


in short, the type of control - common, hybrid, or system specific - will determine who is responsible for the control




