all the decisions made during the initial control selection, tailoring, and supplementation processes will be formally documented to provide a record of the reasons specific controls were selected, expected performance metrics, and their intended use.


the information is essential when the system owner is reviewing the overall security and privacy considerations for systems with respect to potential mission or business case impact. without comprehensive documentation of the reasons for the selection of controls, the understandings, assumptions, and rationale supporting those important risk decisions may not be available when the state of the systems or environments of operation change, and the original risk decisions are revisited.


attention should be paid to the inheritable controls (common or hybrid) to ensure that the system portion of the control implementation statements is complete and addresses the selection controls/control enhancements


the security and privacy plans prepared by the system owner provide an overview of the security and privacy requirements for the system and describe the controls in place or planned for to meet the security and privacy requirements


alternately, organizations may develop a consolidated plan that incorporates security and privacy plans. this may simplify the preparation of the plans, auditing activities, or stakeholder review of the proposed system. 


given the complex nature of most systems, it is vital that stakeholders understand the implications of the systems controls on the part of the system that affects their interests. depending on organizational practice, stakeholder agreement with plans, while desirable, may not be necessary or even possible. however, to not engage stakeholders appropriately is inexcusable, breaching the trust that exists between the stakeholders and system owner


an independent review of the security and privacy plans by the authorizing official may require support from many parts of the organization


depending on the system and organizational practice, the risk executive (function), with support from the senior accountable official for risk management or risk executive (function), CIO, CISO, or chief privacy officer can help determine whether plans are complete, consistent, and satisfy the states security and privacy requirements for the system. the security and privacy plan review also helps to determine whether the proposed controls are effective for their particular purpose. this may be an iterative process as changes may be required.


once satisfied that recommended changes to the security and privacy plans are sufficient, the authorizing official approves the plan. by approving the security and privacy plans, the authorizing official agrees to the set of security and privacy controls (system specific, hybrid, or common) proposed to meet the security and privacy requirements for the system. this approval allows the risk management process to advance to the next step in the process, which is implementation



