align the classification and categorization with baseline controls.


this process is straightforward. the system owner must determine the system classification and document the decision before selecting the controls baseline. this aligns the system with the organizations classification schema


the organization will set its security baseline controls to align with the classification schema requirements. selecting the appropriate security baseline controls aligns the system classification with the security baseline controls.


the process for selecting the categorization controls follows a similar course. the system owner has determined the categorization of the system based on the organizations categorization schema, documenting the decision properly.


the controls must be evaluated to determine how they interact with the proposed system and whether, as defined in the controls catalog, they will meet the control objectives for the system. this requires well defined system requirements.


systems engineering and requirements development: organizational security objectives, organizational security policy, system security policy/personnel security policy, configuration: definitions that determine the appropriate behavior for system elements to achieve the authorized and intended behaviors and outcomes. procedure: definitions that determine the appropriate behavior for personnel who use and sustain the system to achieve the authorized and intended behaviors and outcomes


systems requirements are developed within the systems life cycle. they must be clearly identified at this stage to ensure the appropriate controls can be selected. systems engineering practices are effective at identifying system objectives, requirements, and measures of success. properly selecting controls requires that systems engineering practices have been followed


systems engineering is a structured, top down process to deconstruct the high level organizational security objectives into organizational security policies


these, in turn, will be refined into system security policies and human security policies, ultimately deconstructing these into system security requirements and procedures that define how the system is to be operated. 


several standards, including ISO 15288:2023 Systems and Software Engineering System life cycle processes, and NIST 800-160, rev. 1, Engineering Trustworthy Secure Systems, describe process and give structure to the activities needed to respond to security requirements and create meaningful, measurable security controls


systems security engineering practices are designed to create trustworthy systems. trust is the belief that an entity (e.g. a system) meets certain expectations and can be relied on. trustworthiness is the demonstration of that ability, and it relies on evidence that supports the claim that an entity is deserving of trust. however, not all trusted systems are trustworthy. the lack of demonstrated ability contributes to risk. as a system is created, requirements and artifacts demonastraq5ting the system meets expectations need to be defined, verified, and validated


when a systems security functionality is assumed but not verified, it is often referred to as "veneer security". veneer security creates a false sense of assurance and increases risk due to the uncertainty of the performance of the security functionality. "compliance is a form of veneer security. while compliance may have an important role in judgments of trustworthiness, compliance based judgments - like other forms of veneer securities - do not suffice as the sole evidentiary bases for assurance and the associated judgments of trustworthiness"


in short, trust, but verify is best practice



