information security continuous monitoring architecture


data collection, storage, analysis, and reporting processes form the architectural core of ongoing communication activities


in addition, standards, baselines, protocols, and methods for the information shared must also be addressed. once defined, systems developed within the architecture can be more easily integrated into the reporting activities


ISCM implementation follows in accordance with the strategy, consistent with the architecture. at the system level, all security control classes and types are monitored, and the information is fed into the analytic platforms for subsequent reporting. these activities are supplemented by monitoring by the Organizational and Mission/Business tiers for controls where the efficiency of collecting telemetry is greater when performed at a higher level.


automation of the ISCM activities is essential to reduce the impact of monitoring on system performance and to make the monitoring more efficient. automation consistently improves monitoring reliability, as automated systems do not need breaks or vacations, not do they rely on human memory to log information


however, this does not eliminate the need for trained staff who understand the architecture and keep it operating properly. they still need to know when the automation of the ISCM processes fail so they can reestablish the automated processes as quickly as possible


once the ISCM program begins to collect data, that information should be properly analyzed and used to inform other activities. patch, vulnerability, availability, and capacity management processes are only a few of the myriad activities that will benefit from the data being collected by the ISCM architecture


the reporting may occur in various ways, too. automated dashboards may be useful, but recurring reports, data feeds, and ad hoc, reporting tools may all find useful places in the reporting ecosystem


validating the information is equally important. proper correlation of data sources must take place to ensure that the correct conclusions are drawn from the available information. in many cases, this requires analysis by knowledgeable and skilled professionals who understand the systems and can better provide the data methods to verify the integrity of the data stores.


in r4eporting the findings, however, it is difficult to strike the right balance between too much and too little information. management will need to invest time and effort to fine tune processes that capture the appropriate level of detail to aid in making decisions across different level.


regular communication must occur between the report generators and report consumers so that the information is used accurately and properly. in too many cases, the underlying data quality, timeliness, or volume is insufficient for the decisions made regarding that data


the information presented to decision makers needs to reflect the risks and potential responses the organization could adopt. in many cases, the changed circumstance may be concerning, but the organization could choose to accept the risk presented. in other cases, mitigation or transference to another entity would be the appropriate response. regardless, the decisions need to be documented so that these can be tracked, and the appropriate controls modified, reports updated to provide more effective information, and individuals held accountable for their decisions


it may take time to respond to the information presented through ISCM process, particularly as the issues are raised to the Business/Mission and Organizational tiers. the response may require the development of a corrective action plan that will identify the control changes required, the responsible party for making the changes, the timeline for implementation, and the reporting processes that will signal progress at completing the work. a Plan of Action and Milestones (POA&M) is commonly used to describe this process, although it may also be referred to as a Remediation Plan, Corrective Action Plan, or by similar terms.


however, the ISCM system will not provide all the information necessary to make decisions. when that is possible, the response can likely be automated as well, coding the managements judgment into the automated response. Security Orchestration and Automated Response systems seek to do just that, creating playbooks for changes in KRIs and KPIs, which adjust con5trols in real time. however the reason management exists is to deal with the unknowns that present themselves


general Colin Powell, former chairman of the U.S. Joint Chiefs of Staff and Secretary of State, developed the 40/70 rule for decision making. this rule advises that a decision should be made when one has between 40% and 70% of the necessary information. deciding with less than 40% information involves excessive speculation, while waiting for more than 70% can delay the decision too long, potentially rendering it ineffective in impacting outcomes


information security continuous monitoring is not a linear process. rather, it is best characterized as an ecosystem, with overlapping parts and responsibilities. outcomes from the process are used to improve the process. each tier must establish continuous process improvement practices to use the actual results of their work to improve future efforts. this requires comprehensive documentation of the information provided, actions taken, and results achieved so that good decisions can be made to improve the effectiveness and efficiency of future actions


organizational culture may often inhibit honest, genuine evaluation of program results


people often fell that being judged for their actions is a negative process - that they will be held up to ridicule, be professionally harmed for honest mistakes and judgments, or second guessed by others. the ability of the organizations leadership to use the information honestly and fairly on program effectiveness is a clear indicator of organizational health.


key considerations and actions involved in the implementation and operation of an information security continuous monitoring (ISCM) architecture include automating ISCM activities to reduce performance im-pact, maintaining trained staff to oversee automated processes, and analyzing collected data for informed decision making



