Information Security Continuous Monitoring: Metrics


once a strategy is in place, it is broken down to improve the specific actions that will be carried out


key performance indicators (KPIs) and key risk indicators (KRIs) must be defined, based on the organizations risk tolerance. this information is used to inform managements decisions, so a clear understanding of the organizations strategic goals and preferred decision making methods can help identify meaningful metrics


Key Performance Indicators (KPIs): measuring metrics require that the controls purpose be defined and that its designed performance is established prior to its implementation. also, the controls effectives must be quantifiable through objective methods, such as observation, sampling, or testing. finally, there must be a method or system to capture the results of the control performance, documenting the actual results, and providing a source of data for subsequent analysis must be implemented. in many cases, the method is integrated into the system itself - for example, its logging and monitoring functions. care must be taken to ensure that the performance information is removed from the system being monitored as quickly as possible to reduce the possibility the information will be manipulated by a bad actor and make the information available for analytic purposes without affecting the performance of the production system. most organizations will do this by establishing a logging platform of varying levels of sophistication (e.g. syslog, Security Event and Information Management (SEIM) platform, or a Security Orchestration and Automated Response (SOAR) environment. typical performance metrics include the following: system uptime: this is the amount of time a system is operational and available for use. it is usually expressed as a percentage of total possible operational time. this information would inform the organizational tier in revising hardware standards, improving business continuity planning, and shaping staffing plans. the same information would be useful at the system level to identify specific hardware or software failures. incident response: the number, impact, time to detect, and time to respond to incidents are all useful measures in determining how well the organization uses the metrics. this may shape planning at the Business/Mission tier by highlighting resource shortages or process bottlenecks. at the Organizational tier, this information may be helpful in determining whether the organization is meeting its compliance obligations. patch management: metrics showing how quickly and effectively the organization applies necessary patches or updates to its systems are useful at all levels. patch management is crucial for maintaining system security and functionality. by consistently monitoring these metrics, an organization can identify trends in its patching processes, spot potential vulnerabilities, and prioritize updates based on risk assessments. furthermore, effective patch management not only protects against known security threats but also ensures compliance with industry standards and regulations, which can help prevent costly penalties and enhance the organizations reputation for reliability and safety. change management efficiency: this measures how well the organization manages changes to its systems and processes. it can include metrics like the number of successful changes, number of failed changes, rollback performance, and time taken to implement changes. compliance rate: measures that identify whether the organization is achieving compliance with its statutory and regulatory obligations are of interest at the system level when a system manages functional data or is a system of record. it can include metrics like the percentage of systems that are compliant with specific standards, or the number of noncompliance issues identified. this information will be useful to the other tiers in shaping the policies and organizational strategies of compliance. user satisfaction: this measures how satisfied users are with the system or service. it can be measured through surveys or feedback forms. specific incidents of low user satisfaction will be useful at the system level to identify specific problems to be fixed, but changes to user satisfaction change over time can indicate to the Organization tier that there is a lack of resources or training, or poor leadership in the implementation of technology services. cost efficiency: measuring the financial efficiency of supporting the infrastructure might include metrics like the cost per incident, or the cost of maintaining the monitoring program compared with the cost of potential issues it prevents



Key Risk Indicators (KRIs): while KPIs evaluate the actual performance of a system against expected performance, KRIs are predictive measures that would signal changes to the threat or vulnerabilities associated with its systems, business, or organization. like KPIs, KRIs should be specific, measurable risks relevant to organizational decision making. however, there should be key risks that signal the organization that action must be taken to address the change. this also suggests that fewer KRIs should be monitored, as too many indicators will compete with management for attention. a well established KRI should have an action plan associated with changes in the level of risk. and established response can be integrated into an organizations automation framework, trained as part of the organizations awareness activities, and resourced in advance. for example, most organizations have well established BC/DR plans. trigger events for the BC/DR invocation are typically identified for organizations 5to begin preparing for the pending disaster. an organization located in an area prone to hurricanes or cyclones might have KRIs for when the weather reports first indicate that a particular hurricane may strike the city where the organizations operations are located. this might trigger the relocation of a small group of key employees to staff up the organizations emergency operations center in a location out of the path of the weather event as part of the business continuity plan. additional KRIs would be established so that as the likelihood of the event increases or decreases, changes to the organizational repones would occur. KRIs are often based on events outside the organizations control. zero day threats, earthquakes, political unrest, or vendor failure are example of circumstances typically outside of organizational controls. while proper site location, supply chain management, and robust patch management are good practices, KRIs focus on those circumstances that suggest the increased likelihood of a risk materializing



a key performance indicator (KPI) identifies how a system has worked, while a key risk indicator (KRI) evaluates changes in the environment. KRIs review environmental changes and, together with KPIs, are used by management to identify areas of change that might require controls adjustment. key management indicators, key history indicators, and key environment indicators are not recognized terms in the body of knowledge




