Controls Tailoring and Scoping Guidance



once clear security and privacy objectives are defined, the controls baseline can be applied to the requirements


many common controls are likely to exist that address specific objectives. in other cases, the controls baseline may specify system specific controls that must be implemented to meet security or privacy requirements. regardless, the controls baseline is unlikely to perfectly align with the business needs of the system, and adjustments to the controls suite is necessary. this process is known as controls tailoring.


using system specific controls may provide more granularity of control


many organizations explicitly leave systems out of common controls in favor of system specific capabilities that may exceed the capabilities of the common controls. these decisions must be made on a case by case basis, but in general, organizations have found more value in using a common control architecture where possible. the decision to use system specific or common controls is often one of the major areas of friction in systems design, implementation, and operation


scoping guidance should be developed for each control baseline to allow flexibility in the degree to which individual controls are implemented. this flexibility may be necessary to address specific risk, technology, infrastructure, and other considerations. in some cases, the control may be modified to change how it is implemented; in other cases, the control in the baseline may be completely inappropriate for the system, and another control must be identified to achieve the control objective. the tailoring activities conducted within the scoping guidance must be fully documented in the system security plan so that traceability between the selected controls, control objectives, and applicable control measures demonstrate the need for tailoring, and that the tailoring has the desired effect on the resulting system. the application of scoping guidance must be reviewed and approved by the authorizing official, and the tailored controls must be properly implemented and verified in the same manner as the unmodified controls from the controls baseline

the distinction between scoping guidance and systems tailoring is often confusing. scoping guidance sets the range within which change can be made and still be compliant withy the system baseline. tailoring is the process of applying scoping guidance to a system and deriving a set of applicable control modifications. NIST 800-18 Guide for Developing Security Plans for Federal Information Systems identifies several specific considerations that would result in changes to the scoping guidance.


Technology Related Considerations: security controls that refer to specific technologies (e.g. wireless, cryptography, public key infrastructure) will only be applicable if those technologies are employed or are required to be employed within the information system. security controls will only be applicable to those components of the information system that typically provide the security capability addressed by the minimum security requirements. security controls that can be either explicitly or implicitly supported by automated mechanisms will not require the development of such mechanisms if they do not already exist or are not readily available in commercial or government off the shelf products. in situations where automated mechanisms are not readily available or technically feasible, compensating security controls, implemented through nonautomated mechanisms or procedures, will be used to satisfy minimum security requirements. 


Public Access Information Systems Related Considerations: security controls associated with public access information systems must be carefully considered and applied with discretion since some of the security controls from the specified security control baselines (e.g. personnel security controls, identification, and authentication controls) may not be applicable to users accessing information systems through public interfaces.


Common Security Control Related Conditions: security controls designated by the agency, as common controls will, in most cases, be managed by an organizational entity other than the information system owner. every control in a security control baseline must be addressed either by the agency through common security controls or by the information system owner. decisions on common control designations must not , however, affect the agency's responsibility to provide the necessary security controls required to meet the minimum security requirements for the information system.


Scalability Related Considerations: security controls will be scalable by the size and complexity of the agency implementing the controls and the impact level of the information system. scalability addresses the breadth and depth of security control implementation. discretion is needed to scale the security controls to the environment of use to ensure a cost effective, risk based security controls implementation approach


Risk Related Considerations: security controls that uniquely support the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of security objectives can be downgraded to the corresponding control in a lower baseline (or appropriately modified or eliminated if not defined in a lower baseline) if, and only if, the downgrading action is consistent with the FIPS 199 security categorization for the corresponding security objectives of confidentiality, integrity, or availability before making to the high watermark; is supported by an agency's assessment of risk; and does not affect the security relevant information within the information system


common controls often do not provide the functions that a system specific control might, allowing for more tailored and precise security measures. ease of implementation is generally an advantage of common controls as they have already been implemented and authorized. while common controls are already authorized when declared as such by the organization, system specific controls must go through the RMF process for each specific system, making simplified authorization less applicable to them. additionally, the shared nature of common controls generally makes them less expensive to operate and maintain comparted to system specific controls, but this is not the primary advantage of using system specific controls
